/*!
    \example simple_subscriber
    \title Simple Subscriber
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \brief Subscribing to a geometry_msgs/PoseStamped message and binding its
    fields into the UI.

    \c{Simple Subscriber} is the receiving counterpart to \l{Simple Publisher}.
    It subscribes to a \c geometry_msgs/PoseStamped topic with a single
    \l{Qt ROS2 geometry_msgs QML Types}{PoseStampedSubscriber} and displays the
    incoming pose — position, orientation (as a quaternion \e and as Euler
    angles), timestamp, and frame — using ordinary QML property bindings.

    \image simple_subscriber/simple_subscriber.png "The Simple Subscriber window showing the latest received pose, its frame and timestamp, and the orientation as both a quaternion and Euler angles."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment and launch Qt Creator from that shell.
        \li Open \c examples/simple_subscriber/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li Run the \l{Simple Publisher} example (or any node publishing
            \c geometry_msgs/PoseStamped on \c /simple_publisher_pose) so there
            is something to receive.
    \endlist

    \section1 Declaring a Subscriber

    As with a publisher, a subscriber is a child of a \c Node. It can be given an
    explicit \c node and its \c topic can be edited live:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.Core
    import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs as Geom

    Node {
        id: rosNode
        nodeName: "simple_subscriber_node"

        Geom.PoseStampedSubscriber {
            id: poseSubscriber
            node: rosNode
            topic: topicField.text

            onMessageReceived: (msg) => ++root.messageCount
        }
    }
    \endqml

    There are two ways to react to incoming data, and this example uses both:

    \list
        \li The \c onMessageReceived handler fires once per message. Here it is
            used only to count messages; it is also the right place for
            imperative logic that must run exactly once per message.
        \li Property bindings onto the subscriber's \c message (and its
            convenience accessors) update automatically whenever a new message
            arrives — no handler required.
    \endlist

    \section1 Reactive Bindings and Convenience Accessors

    The subscriber exposes the last message as \c message, and a stamped
    subscriber also surfaces shortcut accessors so common paths read cleanly. For
    a \c PoseStampedSubscriber, \c{poseSubscriber.pose} and
    \c{poseSubscriber.header} are equivalent to \c{poseSubscriber.message.pose}
    and \c{poseSubscriber.message.header}:

    \qml
    // These value-type properties re-evaluate every time a message arrives.
    property Geom.point position: poseSubscriber.pose.position
    property Geom.quaternion orientation: poseSubscriber.pose.orientation

    Label { text: `x=${root.position.x}  y=${root.position.y}  z=${root.position.z}` }
    Label { text: `frame: ${poseSubscriber.message.header.frameId}` }
    \endqml

    \section2 Euler Angles for Free

    A \c geometry_msgs/Quaternion value type also exposes its orientation as
    Euler angles, so you do not have to convert by hand. \c rpyDegrees gives
    roll/pitch/yaw in degrees (there is a matching radians accessor):

    \qml
    Label {
        text: `roll=${orientation.rpyDegrees.x}°  ` +
              `pitch=${orientation.rpyDegrees.y}°  ` +
              `yaw=${orientation.rpyDegrees.z}°`
    }
    \endqml

    \section1 Connection Status

    Alongside \c{rosNode.initialized}, a subscriber reports whether a publisher
    is present on its topic through the \c connected property. The example uses it
    to show "Publisher available" versus "Waiting for publisher", which makes it
    obvious whether the absence of data is a connection problem or simply that
    nothing has been published yet.

    \sa {Simple Publisher}, {Qt ROS2 geometry_msgs QML Types}
*/
